        "thinking": "I'm trying to recall whether सङ्केत belongs to the adanta root list in curādi, checking it against other similar roots like सङ्ग्राम and स्तोम in the Siddhanta Kaumudi.\n\nI'm recalling the sutra listing including \"sañketa grāma guṇa kūṇa āmantraṇe\" as another adanta root, confirming sañketa ends in short a. Then I'm working through forming the luṅ (aorist) of this curādi root, applying the ṇic causative, the ato lopa rule, and checking whether caṅ triggers upadhā hrasva for adanta roots like this one.\n\nSince the a-lopa counts as sthānivat before the vowel, no hrasva or sanvadbhāva applies here because of the specific rule exempting aglopi roots, similar to how kath forms acakathat — so by analogy saṅketa should reduplicate to something like acaṅketat.\n\nI'm trying to recall parallel examples with multi-syllable roots like kumāra or gaveṣ to figure out how the reduplication pattern works for saṅketa, considering whether the sthānivat treatment of a-lopa affects which syllable gets doubled.\n\nConfirming the parasmaipada luṅ form gives अससङ्केतत, with ṇic deleted per the caṅ rule. Though I'm second-guessing the spelling since the dhatu is listed as सन्केत — the dataset might expect अससन्केतत with that nasal spelling instead of अससङ्केतत, so I need to be careful about which convention matches.\n\n",
